 \begin{abstract}
-A fixed-partition L2 may copy a dirty victim into a writeback buffer even though its data do not change. Elastic Payload L2 (EP-L2) retains the victim in place, separates tag visibility from payload ownership and writeback ordering, and allocates resident/writeback roles across a shared pool. We evaluate this WB-only mechanism against a fixed-partition reference with equal payload capacity and modeled bank service. Independent intake of five complete native nonatomic workload pairs confirms removal of all resident-to-writeback copies. However, the geometric-mean execution-speed ratio is only 1.001600, with one slowdown; end-to-end performance is nearly unchanged. Three pressured workloads eliminate WB-data-full events while increasing downstream queue-admission denials. These observations motivate a controlled downstream-headroom study rather than establish its cause. The evidence covers symbolic sector versions and resource lifecycle, not numerical SASS outputs. Independent elasticity benefits, general workload speedup, and physical PPA advantages remain unestablished.
