-\subsection{Causal Limits and Next Test}
-B16 versus U-WB16 combines copy removal and cross-role allocation. The counters demonstrate neither an independent capacity-performance benefit nor the best static partition. The proposed 1024/112/32 resident/bypass/WB control preserves 1168 slots, but requires new quota support and independent qualification; merely selecting WBUF32 in the frozen interface changes the budget. WBUF8 instead uses 1160 slots and is not this control.
-
-The immediate proposed diagnostic is a matched B16/U-WB16 $\times$ original/larger L2-to-DRAM-queue comparison for Convolution and FWT, with CFD retained as an ordering counterexample. It needs separate configuration/resource approval and qualification before execution. Increased headroom changes buffering, not DRAM bandwidth; the speed-ratio interaction must be considered alongside queue occupancy, scheduler service, WAD pressure, and actual bytes. Ideal WB/metadata/read-hit matrices are also unqualified for this production binary. The present WB-only application-speedup case is weak; further experiments should test this ambiguity rather than seek more favorable inputs.
